---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Minetrarchi"
  subtitle="Learning traditional Chinese architecture by taking it apart—and building it again."
  description="A Minecraft-based immersive sandbox concept for learning traditional Chinese architecture through construction and play."
  period="2023"
  kind="Game System, UX & Service Design"
  status="Comprehensive concept design"
  role="User research & personas, game mechanics, art & UI design"
  projectClass="portfolio-page--mine"
  nextHref="/work/expocraft"
  nextTitle="ExpoCraft"
>
  <section class="project-hero project-hero--mine">
    <div class="project-hero__inner">
      <img
        class="project-hero__figure"
        src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m-hero.png"
        alt="Exploded axonometric of a traditional timber building"
      />
      <p>
        The building is not an image. It is a sequence of relations that can be learned by making.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      function setAnchors() {
        var h1 = document.querySelector('.portfolio-identity h1');
        var hero = document.querySelector('.project-hero--mine');
        if (!h1 || !hero) return;
        var stmt = hero.querySelector('.project-hero__inner > p');
        if (!stmt) return;
        var hr = hero.getBoundingClientRect();
        var topGap = hr.top - h1.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
        var bottomGap = hr.bottom - stmt.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
        hero.style.setProperty('--hero-anchor-top', Math.max(0, topGap) + 'px');
        hero.style.setProperty('--hero-anchor-bottom', Math.max(0, bottomGap) + 'px');
      }
      setAnchors();
      window.addEventListener('resize', setAnchors);
      window.addEventListener('load', setAnchors);
    })();
  </script>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>02</span><h2>Heritage Knowledge Stays on the Surface</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement portfolio-statement--small">
      A building is history made visible. Popular encounters stop at its surface.
    </p>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      Traditional architecture is a witness you can see and touch. But as cities accelerated, the
      craft aged with the buildings: skills are being lost, materials are harder to find, and the
      young are slowly forgetting the heritage.
    </p>
    <div class="project-overview-grid project-overview-grid--three">
      <div>
        <h3>Knowledge stays on the surface</h3><p>
          Popular encounters stop at dynasties, ornament and appearance; the structural logic stays
          out of reach.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Sites cannot always be entered</h3><p>
          Preservation, repair and visitor management limit physical access to interiors and
          construction details.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Media explains, but rarely lets you act</h3><p>
          Books, documentaries and museums give information, but no room to assemble, fail, compare
          and rebuild.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Why the timing is right — PEST</h3>
    <div class="pest-grid">
      <article>
        <small>Political</small>
        <h3>Heritage is a supported direction</h3>
        <p>
          The state increasingly values education and protection of traditional buildings; design
          services are a supported industry, and digitising heritage resources is a mainstream
          trend.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Economic</small>
        <h3>A large game market in transition</h3>
        <p>
          The economy is shifting toward quality growth, and the game market is huge—but it needs
          products that focus on player needs and create real value.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Social</small>
        <h3>Curiosity without understanding</h3>
        <p>
          Young people love traditional architecture inside game scenes, yet their knowledge stays
          shallow, and public output is often formulaic or even misleading.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Technological</small>
        <h3>Games as a cultural channel</h3>
        <p>
          Games cross language and cultural boundaries; serious games turn knowledge into skill,
          and open worlds plus VR/AR/MR expand what a builder can do.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head"><span>03</span><h2>Interest Was Not the Problem</h2></div>
    <div class="mine-research">
      <div><strong>70</strong><span>questionnaires</span></div>
      <div><strong>10</strong><span>depth interviews</span></div>
      <p>
        Desk research, questionnaires and interviews pointed to the same gap: curiosity was not the
        problem. People wanted a relaxed route into architectural knowledge—but had no way to learn
        by doing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="research-signal-grid">
      <article>
        <strong>79%</strong><p>
          accepted the claim that traditional timber buildings are built without a single
          nail—memorable folklore replacing structural understanding.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>63%</strong><p>learned through documentaries or popular-science programmes.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>61%</strong><p>
          through museums or exhibitions; books and offline visits were used less consistently.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>60%</strong><p>
          had met access restrictions around historic buildings, maintenance or protected interiors.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="research-signal-grid research-signal-grid--mini">
      <article>
        <strong>27%</strong><p>
          could correctly identify a specific traditional building and explain something about it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>33%</strong><p>
          were misled by modern imitations of ancient architecture into misunderstanding the real
          thing.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>40%</strong><p>
          carried strong impressions from Japanese and Korean building imagery rather than local
          heritage.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-caption">
      Questionnaire snapshot: play happens mostly on mobile (phone 67% · PC 41% · tablet 34% ·
      console 11% · others 7%), which shaped the multi-platform brief from the start.
    </p>
    <div class="persona-card">
      <div class="persona-card__identity">
        <small>Persona</small>
        <h3>钱采薇 (Qian Caiwei)</h3>
        <p class="persona-card__role">Leisure traveller · Game enthusiast</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Gender</dt><dd>Female</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Age</dt><dd>21</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Occupation</dt><dd>University student</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Tianjin</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <ul class="persona-card__traits">
          <li>Quiet</li><li>Shy</li><li>Plenty of free time</li><li>Home-oriented</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="persona-card__voice">
        <small>What she said</small>
        <blockquote>
          “A game could let me understand the building by making it myself, rather than only reading
          an explanation—even as if I had travelled to the site.”
        </blockquote>
        <ul>
          <li>Attracted by the Tang-style scenes of a TV series, she collected books on Tang
            architecture—then fell asleep after two pages.</li>
          <li>She has visited the Forbidden City, the Temple of Heaven and Yu Garden, but remembers
            mostly that they were good for photos.</li>
          <li>She enjoys games and achievement; shy in real life, happy to make friends online.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="quote-pair">
      <blockquote>
        “Traditional architecture may need more scientific knowledge than I expect; I would look for
        an accessible introduction first.”
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        “I have no time to visit the site, and when I do, I only walk through it—I would rather
        experience the beauty of ancient buildings without leaving home.”
      </blockquote>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-caption">
      Interview statements are translated and condensed from the original project portfolio for this
      web presentation.
    </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>04</span><h2>The Core Move: Knowledge Becomes Rules of Play</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement">Turn architectural knowledge into rules of play.</p>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The opportunity was not to explain buildings better. It was to turn architectural knowledge
      into a construction grammar—components, order, proportion and craft—that players assemble
      themselves. Instead of treating a traditional building as a façade to recognize, the design
      lets players take it apart and build it again: guided assembly teaches, free creation extends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head"><span>05</span><h2>Why Minecraft</h2></div>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      Popular science is usually one-way: watch, read, listen. The audience stays passive and the
      learning stays shallow. Serious games flip that—knowledge is generated by doing, and skill
      grows through play.
    </p>
    <div class="reason-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01</span><h3>Construction grammar</h3><p>
          Blocks, modular components and spatial rules turn structure into something players can
          manipulate directly.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span><h3>Low entry, high ceiling</h3><p>
          Suitable for every age above ten—80% of children play Minecraft in multiplayer mode—and
          still deep enough for elaborate buildings and long-term mastery.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span><h3>A culture of circulation</h3><p>
          141 million active players, 500,000 concurrent, education edition in 115 countries, 201
          billion views—maps, mods and tutorials already move through this community.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Where other serious games stopped</h3>
    <div class="toolchain-grid toolchain-grid--four">
      <article>
        <h3>Construction & city sims</h3><p>
          Cities: Skylines, SimCity, Tropico—deep systems, but heavy rules and little heritage
          content.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Sandbox creation</h3><p>
          Minecraft, Terraria, LEGO Worlds, Roblox—huge freedom and communities, but no architectural
          knowledge built in.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Interactive science</h3><p>
          Craft-first titles such as 匠木 (mortise-and-tenon assembly), 重檐 (roof framing) and
          江南百景图 (The Legend of Jiangnan)—thematically right, yet small platforms and limited
          play depth.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>The gap</h3><p>
          Current serious games suffer from weak interaction, heavy mechanics and small platforms.
          Minecraft is the base with both reach and adaptability.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-copy">
      The proposal adapts rather than replaces Minecraft. The base game supplies movement, building
      and multiplayer conventions; Minetrarchi adds architectural components, guided tasks,
      knowledge feedback, assets, progression and a service layer.
    </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>06</span><h2>Three Spaces Form One Learning Loop</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="world-grid world-grid--three">
      <article>
        <small>Guided world</small><h3>Artisan Continent<br /><em>工匠大陆</em></h3><p>
          A task square where builders learn: role-based construction tasks, guidance, explanations
          and reusable components, progressing from simple regional buildings to complex palace
          architecture.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Open world</small><h3>Dawn Plain<br /><em>晨曦之原</em></h3><p>
          A sharing community: earn or exchange assets, build freely, browse community work, trade
          resources and publish your own architectural worlds.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Final chapter</small><h3>Navigator's Realm<br /><em>领航者之境</em></h3><p>
          The ultimate chapter for “building masters”. Completing it creates new learning boards for
          Artisan Continent—the community extends the curriculum.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="rank-strip" aria-label="Rank progression">
      <article>
        <small>Rank 01 · Entry</small>
        <strong>Builder</strong>
      </article>
      <span class="rank-strip__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <article>
        <small>Rank 02 · Advanced</small>
        <strong>Master Builder</strong>
      </article>
      <span class="rank-strip__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <article>
        <small>Rank 03 · Ultimate</small>
        <strong>Architecture Master</strong>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-caption">
      Each of the first two ranks splits into beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, with five
      stars per level. Titles such as 搭建狂魔 (Building Maniac — 50,000 components placed) reward
      long engagement.
    </p>
    <div class="learning-loop">
      <span>Enter a task</span><span>Learn a component</span><span>Assemble</span><span
        >Receive feedback</span
      ><span>Complete and earn</span><span>Build freely</span><span>Share or exchange</span>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">The system in six parts</h3>
    <div class="method-architecture">
      <article>
        <small>Space</small><h3>Maps & physics</h3><p>
          Multi-space worlds and the Minecraft physics engine as the ground for every building
          grammar.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Tasks</small><h3>Story & episodes</h3><p>
          From a simple-structure tutorial to single buildings and finally gardens and complexes;
          expert-authored and user-made maps.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Achievement</small><h3>Visible progress</h3><p>
          Experience, levels, titles and honours that make mastery legible—and unlockable community
          content.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Assets</small><h3>Components & market</h3><p>
          Modules, painted ornaments, sculpture, decorations, trees and drawings—stored, traded and
          reused across spaces.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Social</small><h3>Co-building</h3><p>
          Friends, multi-player collaboration, leaderboards and shared builds keep the loop social
          rather than solitary.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Peripheral</small><h3>Beyond the screen</h3><p>
          Model export, fabrication and links to real scenic areas and VR experience stores.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Four kinds of value</h3>
    <div class="value-grid">
      <article>
        <small>Social value</small><h3>Pass heritage forward</h3><p>
          Promote the intangible heritage of 榫卯 (mortise-and-tenon) joinery, cultural identity
          and confidence, and
          architecture education.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Experience value</small><h3>Learn by making</h3><p>
          A block-and-puzzle approach gives immersive encounters and a deeper feel for architectural
          beauty and construction.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Emotional value</small><h3>Resonance and pride</h3><p>
          Spark interest in traditional buildings, awaken emotional resonance, and build cultural
          belonging.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Functional value</small><h3>Edutainment that works</h3><p>
          An interactive popular-science medium that trains spatial thinking and creativity, then
          connects to real tourism and culture.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The service layer closes the loop: free scenic-area visits, model making, VR experience
      opportunities, knowledge interaction and deeper learning connect the game to real places,
      media, communities and experience stores.
    </p>
    <figure class="project-figure project-figure--white project-figure--titled">
      <div class="project-figure__inner">
        <h3 class="project-figure__title">Game Architecture & Value Network</h3>
        <div class="project-figure__duo">
          <img
            src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m06a.png"
            alt="Game architecture diagram: core gameplay and six systems"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m06b.png"
            alt="Value network and service design map"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>07</span><h2>A Tutorial Is a Construction Sequence</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement portfolio-statement--small">
      The original flow turns a one-bay hall into a sequence of situated decisions rather than a
      diagram to memorize.
    </p>
    <div class="construction-steps">
      <article>
        <span>01</span><h3>Set the plinth</h3><p>
          Read the footprint, position and proportion of the base.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span><h3>Mark the column grid</h3><p>
          Place reference points before placing components.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span><h3>Raise columns correctly</h3><p>
          Ghost guides show location and orientation; the system catches hollow or misassembled
          columns.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span><h3>Connect the frame</h3><p>
          Beams and bracket sets are introduced as load-bearing relations, not decoration.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>05</span><h3>Complete the roof</h3><p>
          Players finish the structural and enclosure sequence across roughly fifteen guided steps.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>∞</span><h3>Use the knowledge</h3><p>
          The completed building, assets and experience move into the free-building loop.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="guide-dialogue">
      <div class="guide-dialogue__head">
        <small>7.2 · Onboarding · Opening script</small>
        <strong>“Welcome to Minetrarchi, Xixi — you are now a builder.”</strong>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Go to Artisan Continent (工匠大陆), where the wisdom of master builders is kept—learn
          the knowledge boards you love, then use them to recreate or create.</li>
        <li>We also hope you become a Master Builder and reach Navigator's Realm (领航者之境), the
          final chapter.</li>
        <li>For builders we have also created Dawn Plain (晨曦之原), a community for sharing
          assets.</li>
        <li>Your Minetrarchi journey begins now. Have a pleasant trip!</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="guide-dialogue__aside">
        Q: Why does a column get damp? A: Moisture in the soil rises as vapour. Set the column on a
        plinth stone (柱础), away from the ground, and it stays dry—knowledge hides in every
        correction.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="project-figure project-figure--scroll">
      <div class="project-figure__stage">
        <img
          src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m07.png"
          alt="Fifteen-step guided building journey"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>STEP 1–15 tutorial flow · scroll horizontally to view.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="portfolio-copy">
      The interface holds game and knowledge together: immediate building guidance, architectural
      explanations, a miniature map, material inventory, task steps and light social presence—
      without splitting the player away from the act of making.
    </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>08</span><h2>The Interface Keeps Game and Knowledge Together</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The interface references Minecraft and makes two moves. It changes the art style to lower the
      UI's presence and raise immersion in the scene. It adjusts the layout to fit the new
      mechanics, work across platforms, and strengthen functionality.
    </p>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Six screens, one language</h3>
    <div class="interface-cards">
      <article>
        <small>Home</small><h3>Choose a space</h3><p>
          New or existing spaces to continue building; profile, friends, assets, and entry to the
          two worlds and settings.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Main</small><h3>Build with context</h3><p>
          Inventory, mini-map, social hints, task steps and prompts stay visible around the scene
          while you build.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Backstage</small><h3>Chat + steps</h3><p>
          Two panels for chatting with co-builders and reviewing the current task and its steps.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Artisan Continent · Tasks</small><h3>Learn by challenge</h3><p>
          Story tasks of increasing difficulty, monthly limited challenges, and the ultimate
          chapter that creates new boards.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Asset Library · Assets</small><h3>Knowledge in the shelf</h3><p>
          Modules, painted ornaments, sculpture, decorations and drawings; hovering shows a short
          knowledge note, plus asset and MOD markets.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Dawn Plain · Community</small><h3>Discuss and share</h3><p>
          A community platform: 太学 (the Academy) boards for architectural knowledge, and a
          交流天地 (exchange corner) for friends and discussion.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Interface screens</h3>
    <div class="placeholder-grid placeholder-grid--six">
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08a.png" alt="Home interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Home</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08b.png" alt="Main interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Main</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08c.png" alt="Backstage interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Backstage</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08d.png" alt="Artisan Continent interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Artisan Continent</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08e.png" alt="Asset Library interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Asset Library</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="project-figure project-figure--item">
        <img src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m08f.png" alt="Dawn Plain interface" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Dawn Plain</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--split">
    <div>
      <div class="portfolio-section__head">
        <span>09</span><h2>One Structure, Three Input Languages</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="portfolio-statement portfolio-statement--small">
        The same actions map across PC, touch and VR—not as three separate products, but as three
        ways to inhabit the same building system.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="input-modes">
      <article>
        <b>PC</b><p>
          Keyboard movement, mouse look, scroll selection and familiar Minecraft shortcuts.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Touch</b><p>
          Direct movement controls, drag-to-look, contextual actions and accessible inventory
          switching.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>VR</b><p>
          Two-hand movement, object selection, menu access and embodied component placement.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figure class="project-figure project-figure--white project-figure--titled">
      <div class="project-figure__inner">
        <h3 class="project-figure__title">Three Input Languages</h3>
        <img
          src="/pictures/work/minetrarchi/m09.png"
          alt="Three-terminal interaction diagrams: PC, touch and VR"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <span>10</span><h2>Beyond the Game: A Cultural Loop</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement portfolio-statement--small">
      The design is built to leave the screen—scenic areas, events, models and communities keep
      the knowledge circulating.
    </p>
    <div class="promotion-grid">
      <article>
        <small>Scenic-area tours</small><h3>Preview the site</h3><p>
          High-level builders make 1:1 maps of scenic areas; visitors tour them in advance, and AR
          checkpoints unlock interactive learning in official saves.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Challenge events</small><h3>Challenge culture</h3><p>
          Join the global building challenges of Minecraft Education and cross-cooperate with
          scenic areas and architecture museums.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Tourism foundation</small><h3>Fund the journey</h3><p>
          A Minetrarchi tourism foundation funds winners and contributors with tickets and travel
          support to experience heritage in person.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Miniature models</small><h3>Print the building</h3><p>
